## P1: the gate merges a rules revert without the marker its subsequent decisions need

At warrant/gate.py:1697-1699, merge_one supplies a custom GitHub merge message for the gate's exact revert, but never puts `warrant-gate revert of <full merge SHA>` in it. The GitHub adapter supplies this exact text as commit_message (warrant/gate.py:1601-1603). The marker open_revert puts on the revert branch's commit remains on the merge's second parent only. reverted() (1256-1257) and reverted_rules() (757-775) search the first-parent merge messages, so they do not see it.

Reproduced with real local Git objects and signed fixture records, invoking the current GT.assess and GT.merge_one. The fixture GitHub adapter implements the requested merge with `git merge --no-ff` and the exact title/message passed by merge_one; no production code is changed. The rules revert is eligible, merge_one reports merged=true and as_assessed=true, but reverted=false and the reverted rules version is not void. The restored .warrant/v3.json then disagrees with the still-effective rules record: a later independently accepted ordinary PR, whose sole refusal is RULES_PROJECTION, is ineligible. This contradicts T's established assertion that the previous rules are in force again after the gate's revert.

Positive control: the identical fixture adds only the machine-readable marker to the simulated merge commit message. reverted=true, the rules version is void, and the later accepted PR has no refusal. No source patch or live GitHub mutation was performed.

The existing RC12 fixture at tests/gate_test.py:1444 manually authors a merge message containing the marker, without calling merge_one. RC12/RC13 and their mutants therefore test a history the production revert merge writer does not generate. A repair should preserve the marker in the first-parent merge commit (or provide an equally authenticated reader/writer contract), and exercise the real merge_one message through the subsequent rules assessment. File the fix and its regression/mutant by an ordinary constitutional PR, as D requires.

